WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Project choice is where a portfolio begins to show judgment.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:11.560
A good project is safe, legal, ethical, relevant to the role you want, and clear enough for someone else to review.

3
00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.660
You do not need dramatic projects.

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.040
You need projects that show useful security thinking.

5
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:24.180
A small, well-scoped detection engineering writeup can be stronger than a large, vague collection of tool screenshots.

6
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:30.760
A carefully documented hardening checklist can be more credible than a project that hints at unauthorized activity.

7
00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.520
Start with the role direction.

8
00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:47.220
If you are aiming for SOC analysis, useful projects might include alert triage notes, sample log analysis, a detection rule with test data, a dashboard using public or synthetic data, or an incident response tabletop report.

9
00:00:47.320 --> 00:01:01.480
For cloud security, projects might include an IAM review in a personal lab, a storage exposure checklist using sample resources, a secure architecture diagram, or a policy baseline explanation.

10
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:10.220
For application security, useful projects can include a threat model, a secure coding fix, a dependency review, or a code review note.

11
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:22.320
For governance, risk, and compliance, projects can include control mappings, risk summaries, policy comparisons, or audit evidence examples built from safe sample scenarios.

12
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.360
Safety is not optional.

13
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:33.740
Use personal labs, intentionally vulnerable practice environments, public sample data, training environments, or systems where you have explicit authorization.

14
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.600
Do not scan random networks.

15
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.180
Do not publish details about real targets.

16
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:46.520
Do not include employer data, customer logs, internal tickets, private screenshots, or sensitive infrastructure details.

17
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:52.620
If a project came from work, assume it is private unless you have clear permission to publish a redacted version.

18
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.220
A clear scope makes projects easier to trust.

19
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.640
Define what is included, what is excluded, and why.

20
00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:08.700
A cloud posture review might cover a small personal account with sample resources, not a real company environment.

21
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:16.080
A vulnerability management workflow might use a lab machine or intentionally vulnerable image, not an unauthorized host.

22
00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.820
A detection writeup might use synthetic logs or public datasets, not private incident data.

23
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.680
Scope protects you and helps the reviewer understand the evidence.

24
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.280
Repeatability also matters.

25
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:32.660
A reviewer should be able to understand how the project was created, even if they do not run it.

26
00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:41.080
Include setup notes when safe, sample inputs, expected outputs, diagrams, references, and limitations.

27
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.380
If a method depends on a tool, explain why the tool was used and what its output means.

28
00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.760
If a result is only a demonstration, say so.

29
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.860
Honest boundaries make a project more professional, not less impressive.

30
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.020
The safest portfolio projects are built to educate.

31
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.200
They show what you can analyze, document, and communicate without risking anyone else's systems or data.

32
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.100
Choose projects that make your target role visible and make your professional judgment easy to see.
